[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-22794"] :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2-\\frac{1}{1+x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}>0\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\ne -1$ the second derivative is always positive since both the numerator and denominator are positive. Therefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ is concave up on its entire domain, $(-1,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-2719/selection/cbf73d33747e7378dc321a3a36a200c81edd3097e93e1a1f09c97cdfaea27c83" :answer/id #uuid "e77c9aae-45aa-549a-bde0-9138bd1adeae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2719/selection/02339682ee49f79f1761eeede3afae21de7104f21b45aa93294c6ddfe52fc68b" :answer/id #uuid "dd5add92-3bcc-5ace-be07-bb89f6fd06ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\infty ,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2719/selection/e58c3f5700f000b0c1af1d73c0dc3d1819c28f20b78f98df84bfa6c1574d32ee" :answer/id #uuid "182fb936-49db-555b-bf5d-51c11841e965" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2719/selection/680ec8692b6fc95fb2227343f14aa1568389f35ada9f43a9c2656345cc979f2a" :answer/id #uuid "cb0c95dc-8676-5961-96b8-e5b9a286c8f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2719/selection/24f4fbc4a7eed36d70a658a0f8157176ca16628f5b872e54061a669ca16c33c0" :answer/id #uuid "c76c6d9d-183f-589f-8653-fd4381ebcde5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function is concave up nowhere." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2719/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4e02abe-fafd-5991-a640-68b2b89b87f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-2719/selection/e58c3f5700f000b0c1af1d73c0dc3d1819c28f20b78f98df84bfa6c1574d32ee" :answer-field/choices ["q-2719/selection/cbf73d33747e7378dc321a3a36a200c81edd3097e93e1a1f09c97cdfaea27c83" "q-2719/selection/02339682ee49f79f1761eeede3afae21de7104f21b45aa93294c6ddfe52fc68b" "q-2719/selection/e58c3f5700f000b0c1af1d73c0dc3d1819c28f20b78f98df84bfa6c1574d32ee" "q-2719/selection/680ec8692b6fc95fb2227343f14aa1568389f35ada9f43a9c2656345cc979f2a" "q-2719/selection/24f4fbc4a7eed36d70a658a0f8157176ca16628f5b872e54061a669ca16c33c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2719" :question/id #uuid "a1508161-8034-5831-a129-903a8a650526" :question/math-academy-id "q-2719" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-9x+6$ is concave up" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-9x+6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6x-9 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ Notice that ${f}^{″}(x)=6>0$ for all values of $x.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2719/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344331/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "0d7edf00-96b3-5ec0-af86-cd6468e0759e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344331/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "628f8670-995e-536c-a534-9a03e1d5940a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344331/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-344331/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344331/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "ba1f896c-e81a-5be4-b7d3-49657a24dec4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344331/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "31cf0912-1e14-556e-a8cc-3fe0e293dd2d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344331/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344331/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344331" :question/id #uuid "4d764786-e782-55ee-95fb-818969f26457" :question/math-academy-id "q-344331" :question/problem "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=7x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=7x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =7-\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}>0\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\ne 2$ the second derivative is always positive since both the numerator and denominator are positive. Therefore, we conclude that the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is $(\\,2,\\infty \\,).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-344331/field-1" "q-344331/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344331"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-2458/selection/e4166d15d0b44d3d165cb3ed8eed855d205982a07de92130f57fc88d75584c2b" :answer/id #uuid "1a451d10-e2af-532f-a4d7-b19989a34adc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2458/selection/053a6843fa060754776b53290c650470848384d571477349d9cbe21300733831" :answer/id #uuid "77f12766-02a7-5d01-9b00-5b711d6e4db3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-\\frac{2}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2458/selection/9d95ecbad09c860b56728fff1815c4b6693e06225af11e65f92786a09cc5fba8" :answer/id #uuid "2677ea65-0bb7-53aa-8ebb-9143c6ec3896" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2458/selection/680ec8692b6fc95fb2227343f14aa1568389f35ada9f43a9c2656345cc979f2a" :answer/id #uuid "56df065e-7270-5318-8aee-81f39434623a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2458/selection/4b474f4e2f607d4a825974a3f3bfcd8e02f477d63408f10e9d9a2cfc1652dd11" :answer/id #uuid "50506176-f62c-555e-a496-23c1d6f3b74e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2458/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "900a2d18-6343-53b5-bfe4-98b15da2ad5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-2458/selection/9d95ecbad09c860b56728fff1815c4b6693e06225af11e65f92786a09cc5fba8" :answer-field/choices ["q-2458/selection/e4166d15d0b44d3d165cb3ed8eed855d205982a07de92130f57fc88d75584c2b" "q-2458/selection/053a6843fa060754776b53290c650470848384d571477349d9cbe21300733831" "q-2458/selection/9d95ecbad09c860b56728fff1815c4b6693e06225af11e65f92786a09cc5fba8" "q-2458/selection/680ec8692b6fc95fb2227343f14aa1568389f35ada9f43a9c2656345cc979f2a" "q-2458/selection/4b474f4e2f607d4a825974a3f3bfcd8e02f477d63408f10e9d9a2cfc1652dd11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2458" :question/id #uuid "61f4c5d5-e624-5f18-a1ff-0dcd8d62b8ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-2458" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-11$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-6x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x+4 & <0 \\\\ 6x & >4 \\\\ x & >\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2458/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2458"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344352/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "480293e6-fc6c-54b6-896a-579231a80508" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344352/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f82ef3b2-4fe9-56d1-92d3-e70dcff0fbbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344352/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer-field/choices ["q-344352/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344352/field-2/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "5b3954b9-d708-5d91-8adc-dfa35c920151" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344352/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "28224180-a635-5de2-9040-4d515801a86c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344352/field-2/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-344352/field-2/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344352" :question/id #uuid "936f4aaf-184a-570f-bb32-26f39de342aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-344352" :question/problem "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{x+4}$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\ne -4.$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{x+4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3(x+4)-(3x+1)}{(x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{(x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =11(x+4)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-22(x+4)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-22(x+4)^{-3} & >0 \\\\ (x+4)^{-3} & <0 \\\\ x+4 & <0 \\\\ x & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\,-\\infty ,-4\\,).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-344352/field-1" "q-344352/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344352"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344777/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "1c4e2c60-9165-5de2-a999-849a8f927e13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344777/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e8ce41-45ed-5eeb-b844-37dbc565c0cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344777/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-344777/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344777/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "8f1ffeb5-af63-59db-b1c7-767c916cdeff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344777/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "997050a4-b25f-5b38-b8db-23b3a0ce17cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344777/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-344777/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344777" :question/id #uuid "2582ee8e-344a-5728-9b4e-ca914cf6725d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344777" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/f03d1f23687273ff94bb2ea4ee45991600c75a237bf4c0a1fdb955d0f00b9b3d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-2$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Find the interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n$f(x)$  is concave down on the interval\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFrom the plotted graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)$, we can  see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval   $(-2,3)$ \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(\\,-2,3\\,).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344777/field-1" "q-344777/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344777"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-31214"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-114060"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/c528a8bdbb9bc6dd1a9895109fdc6818b70787c5024c78e06e2d2ec3997777d6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-31221/selection/d58074533c46cda3b9b4f0b23de9376470c2bc81bb173017e308b38cfe9a5254" :answer/id #uuid "46016a84-8d64-5e42-b17f-5926e610ce6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-5,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31221/selection/92ceb4efdc0cd986fcf6d914c334e6bc24d60c60afe74f5d19105